From 9e84fb646c6c2e66e3be0f0607835469e340dc29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Message-Id: <mft-platform-decoders@streamary.invalid>
From: Streamary <noreply@streamary.invalid>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mft: decode H.264, HEVC, AAC and E-AC3 with Windows' decoders

Streamary decodes the formats whose patents are still in force with Windows' own Media Foundation decoders and
leaves every other format to FFmpeg/mpg123 (the module declines them without an error message).

- H.264/HEVC from MP4/MKV: the avcC/hvcC parameter sets are sent in front of the first packet (and again after a
  flush); before, Microsoft's H.264 decoder took every packet and never output a picture.
- HEVC (MFVideoFormat_HEVC, the HEVC Video Extensions decoder). That decoder crashes in
  MFT_MESSAGE_NOTIFY_BEGIN_STREAMING without MF_MT_FRAME_SIZE, so without a frame size (a live TS) the module opens
  unconfigured and drops the stream until the packetizer finds the size and restarts the decoder. 10-bit streams come
  out as P010.
- E-AC3 (MFAudioFormat_Dolby_DDPlus).
- Every ready output is taken after each input (one per input made frames late, and they were dropped).
- A flush on seek (there was none).
- Low-latency mode for H.264 and HEVC (VLC's start wait gives up on a decoder that holds many frames).
- No decoder installed for a format (e.g. HEVC without the extension) is a warning, not an error.
- The output planes are read with the decoder's own layout (MF_MT_DEFAULT_STRIDE, and MF_MT_FRAME_SIZE, which is
  padded: 1088 lines for 1080p H.264, 192 for 180) instead of the visible size; before, the chroma of such frames sat
  below their luma (colour bleeding at edges, garbage in the top rows).

(libvlcjni checks this line: Message-Id: <mft-platform-decoders@streamary.invalid>)
